What happens next
Organisers will listen, review what happened and decide on a response. Depending on the situation, this may include:

a private conversation or a warning;
removal from an event, chat group or online space;
a temporary or permanent ban from community activities;
in serious cases, involving venue security or the authorities.

We will let the person who reported know what action we took, where appropriate.

Volunteer
The community is run by volunteers. We’d love help with:

planning and running events;
teaching or assisting at workshops;
social media and design;
this website.

Even an hour a month makes a difference. Email TODO-community-email@example.org to get started.

Partner with us
Universities, companies, research institutes, NGOs and innovation hubs can support the community by:

hosting a meetup or workshop at your venue;
sponsoring refreshments, internet or prizes;
sharing internships, jobs and data challenges with members.

Get in touch at TODO-community-email@example.org.

Why a community for R users in Tanzania?
R is free, open-source software for statistics, data analysis and visualisation. Across Tanzania, people already use it to analyse survey and health data, study crop yields and rainfall, write theses and build dashboards. But many of us learn alone, searching online late at night for the meaning of an error message.
We think learning is faster, and a lot more fun, when we do it together. This community is a place to:

meet other people who use R, at your level and in your field;
learn through talks, hands-on workshops and study groups;
share code, tutorials, data sources and job opportunities;
grow a stronger data culture in Tanzania, one analysis at a time.
